WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:12.230
Now I'd like to introduce
you to our speakers today.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:17.010
Our first speaker is Eric Shaqfeh, who
is the Lester Levi Carter Professor and

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:20.330
Department Chair of Chemical Engineering
at Stanford University.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:23.920
He earned a BSE Summa **** Laude
from Princeton University.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:30.180
And a MS and PhD from Stanford University,
all in Chemical Engineering.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:33.060
He joined the Stanford Chemical
Engineering Faculty in early 1990.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.510
And in 2001 he received
a dual appointment, and

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:39.440
became professor of
mechanical engineering.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.280
He is most recently a faculty member
in the Institute of Computational and

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.780
Mathematical Engineering at Stanford.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:48.620
He has authored or
co-authored over 180 publications, and

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:52.970
has been an associate editor of
The Physics of Fluids since 2006.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:56.310
Shaqfeh has been awarded
the APS Francois and Frankiel award,

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:59.640
the NSF Presidential Young Investigator
award, the David and

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.102
Lucile Packard Fellowship in Science and
Engineering, among many others.

00:01:03.102 --> 00:01:05.100
He is a fellow of
the American Physical Society, and

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.590
a member of the National Academy
of Engineering.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.480
Lisa Hwang is a senior lecturer in
the chemical engineering department, and

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.970
is co-academic director of
the Stanford chemical engineering

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:15.920
online master's degree program.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.110
She received her B.S. in chemical
engineering from MIT, and her MS and

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.540
PhD in chemical engineering from Stanford.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.572
Working with the Kurt Frank group on
polymer thin foams and interfaces.

00:01:25.572 --> 00:01:28.910
She has served as a lecture consultant for
the Office for the Vice for Teaching and

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:31.540
Learning, and
is the faculty director of TA training for

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:33.240
the chemical engineering department.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.880
She has been teaching in
the department since 2006.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.410
Elizabeth Sattely is an Assistant
Professor in the department of

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:43.260
Chemical Engineering at Stanford, and
a Stanford ChEM-H faculty fellow.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.000
She also serves as an honorary
adjunct staff scientist,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.480
at the Carnegie Institution of Science.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.180
Doctor Sattely completed her graduate
training at Boston College in

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.290
Organic Chemistry, and

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:55.890
her post-doctoral studies in
Bio-Chemistry at Harvard Medical School,

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:59.430
where she worked on natural
product biosynthesis in bacteria.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:03.030
Inspired by human reliance on plants and
plant derived molecules for medicine,

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.190
the Sattely laboratories focused on
the discovery and engineering of plant

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:10.820
metabolic pathways, to make molecules
that can enhance human and plant health.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:14.680
Work in the Sattely lab has been
recognized by a NIH New Innovator Award,

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:19.540
a DOE Early Career Award,
An HHMI Simons Faculty Scholar Award,

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:24.010
and an AAAS Mason Award for
Women in the Chemical Sciences.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:27.300
And finally, Alexander Dunn is an
Associate Professor in the Department of

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.610
Chemical Engineering at
Standford University.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:33.840
His research focuses on understanding how
living cells sense mechanical stimuli,

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.775
with particular interests in stem
cell biology and tissue engineering.

00:02:36.775 --> 00:02:41.080
Dr. Dunn worked as
a post-doctoral scholar with Jane

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:45.180
in the department of bio-chemistry at the
Stanford University school of medicine.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:48.030
He received his PhD at
the California Institute of Technology

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:51.420
under the direction of Harry Grey,
where his work focused on understanding

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:57.660
the catalytic mechanisms selected
CH bond oxidation by P450 enzymes.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:01.250
His work has been recognized with numerous
awards including the Hertz fellowship,

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.230
Jane Coffin Childs
postdoctoral fellowship,

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:06.390
the Burroughs Welcome Career Award
at the Scientific Interface, and

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:08.450
NIH director's New Innovator Award.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:10.980
So, an amazing panel that we have today.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:15.538
So, now I'd like to start by turning
the floor over to Eric and Lisa.

00:03:19.422 --> 00:03:24.015
All right, today we're here to discuss
the introduction of a new online

00:03:24.015 --> 00:03:28.834
Master's Degree in Chemical Engineering,
and I would like to give you some

00:03:28.834 --> 00:03:33.070
history and perspective On how
that came about in our department.

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:36.480
We've had a residential masters for
many years now.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.100
Even near the beginning of the department.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:42.560
Our department is somewhere
around 50 plus years old.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.730
But we all thought that we actually
missing an audience with our masters

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:51.750
program, particularly the people who are
professionals working at companies that

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:57.160
wanted to get advanced education, but
essentially were working full time jobs.

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:01.740
So we wanted to open the access to this,
and

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:06.680
we now have a faculty that's of that size
that we can actually do such a thing,

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:09.350
so this is the introduction
of a new program.

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:11.090
We're targeting a new audience,

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:16.790
we have our own new admissions program for
that degree.

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:21.526
And we'd like to talk to you about some of
the specifics so that we encourage you,

00:04:21.526 --> 00:04:25.915
if you have this interest, to apply
to our online master's degree program

00:04:25.915 --> 00:04:28.580
now through March 19th for this go around.

00:04:31.643 --> 00:04:35.211
Ultimately, our program was structured
with these two goals in mind,

00:04:35.211 --> 00:04:37.473
to provide greater access, as I mentioned,

00:04:37.473 --> 00:04:40.750
to a population perhaps we
weren't reaching for before.

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:45.480
And also, Stanford is an amazing place
where you can structure a degree,

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:49.000
and we're giving you the flexibility
to structure that degree, in a way that

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:54.240
combines a very internationally top-ranked
chemical engineering institution,

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:57.720
with a wide range of engineering
coursework that's offered at Stanford.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:02.740
So you can structure a program
within your interest set.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:07.875
With an interest set that perhaps has
interests outside chemical engineering,

00:05:07.875 --> 00:05:12.938
such as entrepreneurship, artificial
intelligence, biological studies,

00:05:12.938 --> 00:05:17.562
etc., and still obtain a very
first-rank online master's program.

00:05:20.535 --> 00:05:24.440
We structured it to accomplish
these goals in the following way.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:27.660
We're going to ask you to
complete 45 academic units,

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:29.370
all offered by the online learning.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:34.170
27 units in chemical engineering,
8 graduate level courses and

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:35.580
one engineering seminar.

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:41.700
And those courses will have
in them not just you folks,

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:47.550
the online masters folks, but also Our PhD
students, our residential masters, etc.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:52.390
And these will be taught by our regular
faculty, all of them, on a regular basis.

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:55.955
Beyond that then, we add the flexibility
that you can, as I said,

00:05:55.955 --> 00:06:00.095
structure the program so you can look
at other interests that you might have,

00:06:00.095 --> 00:06:04.000
that will be supplementary,
to you interest in chemical engineering.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.458
And that means that you can take 18
units in engineering, science, or

00:06:08.458 --> 00:06:11.896
math electives,
200-level classes or above, for

00:06:11.896 --> 00:06:16.450
any of the online offerings that
are presently offered within Stanford.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:20.390
Lisa, who's the director
of the program and

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:24.190
myself are going to review
your program sheets.

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:28.940
And particularly we're going to look
at programs which have three or

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:30.890
four electives in a single topical area.

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:35.050
Again, that's part of our programming
when people who have to combine

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:39.480
the chemical engineering,
with an area that they are interested in.

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:43.130
And then two or
three other electives that might be

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:46.920
