00:13:11.490 --> 00:13:14.530
So I'll begin by just
mentioning in a few minutes my

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:19.890
research lab's interests, and
that is really very simple.

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:26.080
You and I and the mouse you see on
your screen all began as a relatively

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:31.960
undifferentiated mass of cells, and yet
we complex and beautiful organisms.

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:35.740
And as an engineer this provokes
a very simple question.

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:38.640
How did that happen, and how can

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:43.120
we recapitulate that process in the lab in
order to build new replacement tissues?

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:45.670
So the goals of my lab

00:13:46.870 --> 00:13:51.400
are to understand how biology
builds itself from molecules up.

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:55.220
And importantly, to understand
the physical rules by which cells work

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:58.490
together to construct living tissues.

00:13:58.490 --> 00:14:01.180
So for example,
in the story we published a few years ago,

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:04.090
we performed simple experiments
on zebrafish embryos,

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:07.120
these are little fish,
just like you'd see in a pet store.

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:10.760
We learned some simple rules about how
the tissues arrange themselves and

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:12.120
we're now applying those rules.

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:16.690
To try to understand how groups of stem
cells work together in the lab in order to

00:14:16.690 --> 00:14:20.520
build a complex tissue that could be
used for regenerative medicine purposes.

00:14:22.450 --> 00:14:26.570
Now as part of that study,
we're really interested in the sensors and

00:14:26.570 --> 00:14:28.670
actuators inside every cell in your body.

00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:34.980
You can think of these as
nanometer-sized string gauges or

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:39.650
even muscles but
scaled down to the nanometer length scale.

00:14:39.650 --> 00:14:46.680
Importantly, these molecular assemblies

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:51.800
actually perform both mechanical
functions and also compute information.

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:55.149
Something I would love to talk
about with you at greater length.

00:14:56.800 --> 00:15:01.820
Now in one such study,
we used advanced light

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:06.420
imaging to try to understand how living
cells move through solid tissues and

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:09.290
how they remodel their
physical surroundings.

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:16.460
And what we found is that these cells
are powered by contractile struts

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:18.990
as you see in this movie.

00:15:18.990 --> 00:15:23.590
Where we used advanced image processing to
track the movement of individual protein

00:15:23.590 --> 00:15:25.620
complexes within a living cell.

00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:29.820
And one way to think about
it is that these cells,

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:35.350
which happen to be the cells that repair
wounds, for instance in your skin.

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:39.480
Actually, it's the same design principles
in order to generate force and

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:42.690
motion is this sort of
creepy looking cheetah robot

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:45.520
that was created a few years
ago by Boston Dynamics.

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:52.976
We are of course here to talk
about the classes that I teach,

00:15:52.976 --> 00:15:57.240
there are two classes, if time allows,
I'll discuss both of them.

00:15:57.240 --> 00:16:02.140
The first which I'm teaching next
quarter is called Chemical Kinetics and

00:16:02.140 --> 00:16:03.170
Reaction Engineering.

00:16:03.170 --> 00:16:08.817
And it tells stories in that class that
leads to the images you see on the screen.

00:16:08.817 --> 00:16:13.638
Now, here is the syllabus, and
our approach to this topic is a little

00:16:13.638 --> 00:16:18.309
bit different in this department
than in this other departments.

00:16:18.309 --> 00:16:22.892
It focuses very much on the fundamentals
that drive chemical reactivity.

00:16:22.892 --> 00:16:28.387
So we start with transition state theory,
the question of how reactions occur.

00:16:28.387 --> 00:16:33.411
And as I'll show you in the next slide,
we also think about the deep connections

00:16:33.411 --> 00:16:38.293
between the kinetics of chemical
reactions and basic statistical physics.

00:16:38.293 --> 00:16:43.559
We then move on to understanding the basic
rules that underlie as modern and

00:16:43.559 --> 00:16:47.671
predictive understanding
of heterogeneous catalysis.

00:16:47.671 --> 00:16:50.299
And explore their deep connections

00:16:50.299 --> 00:16:54.952
with the same rules that control
how biological enzymes work.

00:16:54.952 --> 00:16:58.954
We then finish with an introduction
to systems biology and

00:16:58.954 --> 00:17:02.153
systems of coupled reactions and
transport.

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:07.021
Now, this is a practical example.

00:17:07.021 --> 00:17:09.327
Here is the Monsanto process,

00:17:09.327 --> 00:17:15.183
which is used in an industrial scale
to produce acetic acid, usually within,

00:17:15.183 --> 00:17:20.438
if my memory serves, an iridium
rather than rhodium based catalyst.

00:17:20.438 --> 00:17:25.260
Now, in this laboratory study, it was
discovered that despite the presence

00:17:25.260 --> 00:17:28.911
of eight species and
corresponding number of reactions.

00:17:28.911 --> 00:17:34.109
The overall rate was dictated by
the concentration when we choose species.

00:17:34.109 --> 00:17:39.207
Using very simple principles, this also
called to determine why that is and

00:17:39.207 --> 00:17:42.404
use that information to
optimize reactivity.

00:17:42.404 --> 00:17:44.427
As I mentioned earlier,

00:17:44.427 --> 00:17:50.219
there really only a few physical
rules that govern chemical kinetics.

00:17:50.219 --> 00:17:54.244
One of them is very simple
statistics of improbable events.

00:17:54.244 --> 00:17:58.616
So in the first lecture of the course,
I'll explain to you why

00:17:58.616 --> 00:18:03.497
the injuries to soldiers due to
poorly behaved horses in the Prussian

00:18:03.497 --> 00:18:08.382
army actually led to the fundamental
mathematical understanding,

00:18:08.382 --> 00:18:12.281
the drives are understanding
of chemical reactions.

00:18:12.281 --> 00:18:17.580
So for example, unlike in most courses
on this topic, we'll actually perform

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:22.345
stochastic reactions to give you
insight on the chemical reactivity.

00:18:22.345 --> 00:18:27.334
And allow you to model system with
small number of components as

00:18:27.334 --> 00:18:32.430
would be common for
instance in some diagnostic applications.

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:37.376
The second topic in this course
is why do reactions happen and

00:18:37.376 --> 00:18:39.899
what makes them faster slope.

00:18:39.899 --> 00:18:45.787
So here is just an example slide showing
one fundamental result in the field,

00:18:45.787 --> 00:18:50.496
transition state theory that
relates the rate of a reaction to

00:18:50.496 --> 00:18:54.954
a ratio of patrician coefficients
Q double dagger and QA.

00:18:54.954 --> 00:18:59.206
This provides deep and beautiful
connections between our understanding of

00:18:59.206 --> 00:19:02.205
statistical mechanics and
of chemical reactivity.

00:19:04.526 --> 00:19:07.808
As the quest progresses we
move on to more modern topics.

00:19:07.808 --> 00:19:11.159
I'm particularly enthusiastic
about de novo design and

00:19:11.159 --> 00:19:15.359
functional enzymes for both laboratory and
industrial application.

00:19:15.359 --> 00:19:20.543
And this is a topic that I think will have
enormous importance in the coming years.

00:19:22.431 --> 00:19:29.048
Now, that's the most relevant and
immediate information.

00:19:29.048 --> 00:19:34.395
I should mention that I also teach a
second online course on Biochemistry, it's

00:19:34.395 --> 00:19:40.392
quite complimentary to the advanced topics
taught by my colleague, Professor Sattely.

00:19:40.392 --> 00:19:45.306
And just like in Kinetics I teach
that course around stories involving

00:19:45.306 --> 00:19:49.233
all of the things you,
You see you on your screen there.

00:19:49.233 --> 00:19:51.702
Now, why study metabolism?

00:19:51.702 --> 00:19:56.768
Which is really the basic concept in
this course, is human metabolism.

00:19:56.768 --> 00:20:02.671
Well, human disease is one very
important and obvious application.

00:20:02.671 --> 00:20:06.858
Perhaps slightly less obvious,
but still pretty immediate,

00:20:06.858 --> 00:20:09.625
is the connection to synthetic biology.

00:20:09.625 --> 00:20:13.754
The rules you'll learn in this
class will allow you to construct,

00:20:13.754 --> 00:20:18.634
in a rational way, complex molecules,
like the antimalarial Artemisinin.

00:20:18.634 --> 00:20:22.768
And then finally this course
provides foundational knowledge for

00:20:22.768 --> 00:20:26.990
understanding how the beautiful,
natural world around us works.

00:20:26.990 --> 00:20:32.407
Now, the underlying insight in this course
is how to go from complex information

00:20:32.407 --> 00:20:37.279
like this metabolic network I'm
showing here to fundamental insight.

00:20:37.279 --> 00:20:42.042
In other words, how to pack detail into
your head in a way that makes sense and

00:20:42.042 --> 00:20:45.629
it allows you to make practical
decisions in the future.

00:20:45.629 --> 00:20:49.180
All right, that's all of my material.

00:20:49.180 --> 00:20:52.540
I can answer a few questions
if you're interested.

00:20:52.540 --> 00:20:53.565
&gt;&gt; We did have a question come in for you,

00:20:53.565 --> 00:20:54.571
Alex.
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:20:54.571 --> 00:20:57.296
&gt;&gt; So someone asked, within your course do

00:20:57.296 --> 00:21:02.189
you explore how small chemical changes
impact IPSCs to form teratomas,

00:21:02.189 --> 00:21:05.995
or how other cell culture errors develop?
&gt;&gt; Yes, indeed, we do.

00:21:05.995 --> 00:21:10.031
So for those of you who may not
know those acronyms, that's

00:21:10.031 --> 00:21:16.196
Induced Pluripotent Stem Cells, which is a
powerful substrate for tissue engineering.

00:21:16.196 --> 00:21:21.187
We're especially interested in how
the interplay between mechanical cues

00:21:21.187 --> 00:21:26.023
like tissue geometry or mechanical
forces within the tissue combine with

00:21:26.023 --> 00:21:31.172
the biochemical signals that the cell
receives in order to tell the cell live,

00:21:31.172 --> 00:21:34.379
die, divide, differentiate or
stay the same.

00:21:34.379 --> 00:21:37.976
These are the rules that construct us and
that I think it would be incredibly

00:21:37.976 --> 00:21:40.444
important to understand for
tissue engineering.

00:21:42.709 --> 00:21:44.101
All right.
&gt;&gt; Great,

00:21:44.101 --> 00:21:49.491
I don't see any other questions
coming through quite yet.

00:21:49.491 --> 00:21:54.329
So if anyone has any other questions for
Alex feel free to put them through.

00:21:54.329 --> 00:21:57.826
But if we don't get a chance to follow up
because I know he needs to go to his other

00:21:57.826 --> 00:22:00.167
commitment we'll certainly
do some follow-ups.

00:22:00.167 --> 00:22:01.782
So thank you so much Alex for

00:22:01.782 --> 00:22:05.506
taking the time today to speak.
&gt;&gt; My pleasure and happy to answer any

00:22:05.506 --> 00:22:07.386
questions you may have.
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:22:07.386 --> 00:22:08.033
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:22:08.033 --> 00:22:09.006
&gt;&gt; All right,

00:22:09.006 --> 00:22:12.906
so now I'm going to move
on to our next speaker.

00:22:12.906 --> 00:22:14.838
I'd like to hand it over to Beth.

00:22:14.838 --> 00:22:19.465
A fun fact about Beth,
she was recently named as one of the 47

00:22:19.465 --> 00:22:23.030
investigators on
the Chan Zuckerburg bio hub.

00:22:23.030 --> 00:22:25.709
Where she will be focusing
merging engineering and

00:22:25.709 --> 00:22:29.459
detailed knowledge of plant biochemical
pathways to enhance health.

00:22:29.459 --> 00:22:33.273
This bio hub initiative will invest
more than $50 million to support people

00:22:33.273 --> 00:22:35.258
like Beth in her life science research.

00:22:35.258 --> 00:22:40.047
So it's an exciting opportunity to take
her course and to learn from her so

00:22:40.047 --> 00:22:41.990
now I'll hand it over to her.

00:22:41.990 --> 00:22:44.683
Thanks Beth.
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks very much, Stephanie for

00:22:44.683 --> 00:22:45.608
the introduction.

00:22:45.608 --> 00:22:52.940
I want to start, by actually introducing
my lab, and my broader research program.

00:22:52.940 --> 00:22:57.014
We're a collection of engineers and
scientists,

00:22:57.014 --> 00:23:00.244
who are interested in plants as plants.

00:23:00.244 --> 00:23:05.655
And what I mean by that, is the ability
of green plants to produce molecules,

00:23:05.655 --> 00:23:09.253
at scale, that are critical for
life as we know it.

00:23:09.253 --> 00:23:13.438
And so we're fascinated by the amazing
capacity of plants to take

00:23:13.438 --> 00:23:18.081
the world's simplest building block,
carbon dioxide, and produce so

00:23:18.081 --> 00:23:22.744
many molecules that are important to us,
for example, the food we eat.

00:23:22.744 --> 00:23:26.752
These processes lead to the air we
breathe, biofuels we're considering for

00:23:26.752 --> 00:23:30.514
renewable energy, as well as numerous
molecules that are either used in

00:23:30.514 --> 00:23:33.489
the clinic or part of our diet
that influence our health.

00:23:33.489 --> 00:23:36.388
And so
plants are already incredibly productive.

00:23:36.388 --> 00:23:39.770
But we realized that modern tools and
genetic engineering,

00:23:39.770 --> 00:23:42.885
which allow us to make multiple
simultaneous changes in

00:23:42.885 --> 00:23:46.747
a complex organism's genome opens
the door for new possibilities.

00:23:46.747 --> 00:23:51.696
So, I want to just introduce three grand
challenges that motivate the research in

00:23:51.696 --> 00:23:52.216
my lab.

00:23:52.216 --> 00:23:56.901
And as I'll tell you, also have
motivated some of the concepts I go

00:23:56.901 --> 00:24:00.679
over in the course I teach
biochemical engineering.

00:24:00.679 --> 00:24:04.820
So these challenges have to do with
altering the metabolism of plants.

00:24:04.820 --> 00:24:09.360
Using these engineering tools, so for
example if we have molecules from plants

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:12.970
that we've used in the clinic,
it's critical to know

00:24:12.970 --> 00:24:16.720
that many of these compounds are produced
by plants that are difficult to cultivate,

00:24:16.720 --> 00:24:20.948
or by plant cell culture, which can be
an inefficient source of these molecules.

00:24:20.948 --> 00:24:24.720
And so we'd like to use engineered
metabolism, or engineered biosynthesis,

00:24:24.720 --> 00:24:29.600
to come up with a new way to
manufacture these particular compounds.

00:24:29.600 --> 00:24:34.090
We're also interested in how the chemistry
of plants influences plant fitness,

00:24:34.090 --> 00:24:37.562
and furthermore how it can be
engineered to enhance plant fitness.

00:24:37.562 --> 00:24:41.150
The small molecules plants produce,
are critical for

00:24:41.150 --> 00:24:46.376
allowing plants to obtain the nutrients
they need to grow and prevent disease,

00:24:46.376 --> 00:24:51.383
and grow in less desirable areas, for
example, high salinity or drought.

00:24:51.383 --> 00:24:55.071
And finally, another area in my lab that
has to do with chemistry of plants and

00:24:55.071 --> 00:24:58.249
human health, has to do with
optimizing nutrition in our diet, so

00:24:58.249 --> 00:25:01.970
really using a metabolic engineering
approach, to make plants healthier.

00:25:03.340 --> 00:25:07.040
And so again, these grand challenges
motivate the work in my group, and

00:25:07.040 --> 00:25:13.090
also how I approach ChemEng
355 Biochemical Engineering.

00:25:13.090 --> 00:25:18.730
So in this course is I'll tell you,
my goal is to really present the tools and

00:25:18.730 --> 00:25:24.160
concepts that are required, to really
build any engineered biological system.

00:25:24.160 --> 00:25:27.210
So as i have shown in the circle
that could be an engineered cell,

00:25:27.210 --> 00:25:30.230
it could be an engineered higher
organism such as a plant.

00:25:30.230 --> 00:25:35.230
But we're going to modify machinery,
from DNA, to RNA, to protein to either

00:25:35.230 --> 00:25:39.940
product or function,
to obtain new chemical functionality.

00:25:39.940 --> 00:25:44.700
And so some of these things you can think
about are maybe ones you've heard before,

00:25:44.700 --> 00:25:48.760
for example the production of antibiotics
in cells, or the production of therapeutic

00:25:48.760 --> 00:25:52.000
proteins, but there are a lot
of new possibilities as well.

00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:55.580
How could we engineer cells
to produce fuels or plastics?

00:25:55.580 --> 00:25:58.160
Or even small molecules that
you don't even find in nature.

00:25:59.210 --> 00:26:04.470
On the function side, again we can think
about using cells as essentially tools and

00:26:04.470 --> 00:26:08.510
machines for information storage,
cell based therapies, and

00:26:08.510 --> 00:26:10.360
even on the whole organism scale again.

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:14.725
For example, non-browning apples is a
recent example from the agriculture side.

00:26:14.725 --> 00:26:22.486
And so within these challenges comes
the framework for the course objectives.

00:26:22.486 --> 00:26:27.826
And so my goal is not to have a collection
of students with an expansive knowledge

00:26:27.826 --> 00:26:33.009
of biology, but to deliver in this course
the basic concepts and the language

00:26:33.009 --> 00:26:39.420
of biochemical engineering, that would
allow you to build any engineered system.

00:26:39.420 --> 00:26:43.230
And so at the end of the course,
once you finish,

00:26:43.230 --> 00:26:47.830
my objective is that Any new process that
you hear about, either in the news or

00:26:47.830 --> 00:26:51.690
a colleague has worked on, or something
that's being developed at a company.

00:26:51.690 --> 00:26:55.480
You understand each of the components
required to build that system from

00:26:55.480 --> 00:26:58.060
scratch, using quantitative principles.

00:26:58.060 --> 00:27:02.270
I'd also like to convey, especially
towards the second half of the course,

00:27:02.270 --> 00:27:05.920
ideas about, what are the limits
of biochemical engineering?

00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:07.910
What new tools might we need to develop?

00:27:07.910 --> 00:27:09.530
And what's on the horizon?

00:27:09.530 --> 00:27:12.640
What are some of the frontiers and
challenges in this field?

00:27:14.570 --> 00:27:17.620
And so, the course is really
organized into two parts.

00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:22.900
In the beginning, we go through some of
the background biology that builds on

00:27:22.900 --> 00:27:26.560
much of the things that Alex,
my colleague, talked about.

00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:31.240
In terms of fundamentals of biological
systems, what aspects of a cell do you

00:27:31.240 --> 00:27:35.670
need to understand, in order to be able
to engineer the chemistry of a cell?

00:27:35.670 --> 00:27:38.030
We do this using a quantitative approach.

00:27:38.030 --> 00:27:42.160
For example, rates in biological systems,
and rates of central dogma, so

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:45.120
that we can really think about
how to apply process and

00:27:45.120 --> 00:27:48.000
engineering controls to get
the output that we're interested in.

00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:50.670
In the second half of the course,

00:27:50.670 --> 00:27:55.510
once we have this understanding
starting from a piece of DNA,

00:27:55.510 --> 00:27:59.440
going to a cell and an organism level,
what are some of the applications?

00:27:59.440 --> 00:28:02.620
So how might we generate
proteins with new function, or

00:28:02.620 --> 00:28:05.260
metabolic pathways with
a different output?

00:28:05.260 --> 00:28:10.220
And then, how do we think about scaling
up and process considerations for

00:28:10.220 --> 00:28:11.720
these applications?

00:28:12.970 --> 00:28:15.900
And so, I want to just close
with one example from my lab,

00:28:17.020 --> 00:28:21.950
which is a complex process, and really,
a challenge that the principles that

00:28:21.950 --> 00:28:25.920
you learned in chemical engineering
355 could help you adjust.

00:28:25.920 --> 00:28:29.910
So, one of the things that we were really
interested in Is an anti-cancer agent,

00:28:29.910 --> 00:28:34.800
Atopicide, that's used in many
clinically applied chemotherapeutics.

00:28:34.800 --> 00:28:38.720
So this is a molecule that's currently
produced from a plant, a medicinal plant,

00:28:38.720 --> 00:28:41.090
that's very difficult to cultivate and
in fact,

00:28:41.090 --> 00:28:45.120
there is often [INAUDIBLE] sources
of this particular molecule.

00:28:45.120 --> 00:28:49.100
So in recent years shortages
have been reported by the FDA,

00:28:49.100 --> 00:28:52.780
in part because we have no good
way of manufacturing this drug,

00:28:52.780 --> 00:28:54.970
beyond isolation from the medicinal plant.

00:28:54.970 --> 00:28:58.520
And so the challenge for
us is could we rebuild biology?

00:28:58.520 --> 00:29:02.020
Could we take that metabolic pathway
from this medicinal plant, and

00:29:02.020 --> 00:29:04.480
engineer it as a way to
produce this compound.

00:29:05.670 --> 00:29:09.290
And so what we needed to do was
identify genes, we needed a source for

00:29:09.290 --> 00:29:11.260
each of these pieces of DNA.

00:29:11.260 --> 00:29:14.770
We needed to control how they
might be expressed in a chassis.

00:29:14.770 --> 00:29:18.500
And we needed to find ways to
keep those genes in our chassis,

00:29:18.500 --> 00:29:22.160
and get to the output of interest.

00:29:22.160 --> 00:29:24.930
And so we've been able to do this
in my lab, take the genes for

00:29:24.930 --> 00:29:29.370
a medicinal plant, and transfer them to
a much easier to cultivate and grow plant,

00:29:29.370 --> 00:29:33.025
Nicotiana which is a wild
relative of tobacco.

00:29:33.025 --> 00:29:36.700
So thinking back to
Biochemical Engineering 355,

00:29:36.700 --> 00:29:40.400
in that course we'll learn about
all the steps of a process.

00:29:40.400 --> 00:29:43.605
Whether you want to take the genes
from a medicinal plant and

00:29:43.605 --> 00:29:47.924
put them into a chassis like tobacco, or
you want to build an engineered T cell or

00:29:47.924 --> 00:29:49.970
some other process in engineering.

00:29:51.510 --> 00:29:52.760
Great, thanks so much Beth.

00:29:52.760 --> 00:29:57.050
So as a reminder, if you have questions
